  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...\nStill no major changes are expected in the long term forecast, as\na high pressure ridge dominates the Gulf for the foreseeable \nfuture, bringing consistently dry and benign weather. Beginning \nSaturday morning, winds across the inner channels and coastal \nwaters are expected to weaken and remain at or below 10 knots. \nAfternoon sea breezes will likely develop with daytime heating, \nbut are not expected to cause significant impacts.\n\nThe greatest uncertainty in the long term is whether a weak \ndisturbance will bypass the northern panhandle early next week. \nModel guidance generally agrees that high pressure will strengthen\nand block most appreciable precipitation, however, there is a \nsmall but increasing chance of light rain reaching the northern \npanhandle/outer coast region by midweek. Highest confidence is \nthat most precipitation will stay over the mainland interior, \nthough could still see widespread cloud cover move in from the \nnorth by Monday. Overall, generally dry conditions with daytime \ntemperatures rising into the mid 60s to low 70s across most of the\nregion, continuing to remain seasonably warm through midweek.",
